Since the sum of the squares on {AB} and {BC} is rational, while twice the rectangle {AB} by {BC} is medial, therefore the sum of the squares on {AB} and {BC} is incommensurable with twice the rectangle {AB} by {BC}, and, in conversion, the sum of the squares on {AB} and {BC} is incommensurable with the remainder, the square on {AC}. [!just II.7; X.16]

But the sum of the squares on {AB} and {BC} is rational, therefore the square on {AC} is irrational. Therefore {AC} is irrational.

Let it be called minor.

<div class="qed">Q.E.D.</div>
